<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gongyang Zhuan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gongyang Commentary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gongyang Zhuan is one of the three classical commentaries on the Spring and Autumn Annals, traditionally attributed to Gongyang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a>, a disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>'s follower Zixia. Written down in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> in question-and-answer form, it reads hidden moral judgements into the wording of the Annals. It was the leading commentary of Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and was revived by Qing reformers such as Kang Youwei.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gongyang-zhuan.jpg" alt="Gongyang Zhuan" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E8%87%B3%E8%81%96%E5%85%88%E8%B3%A2%E5%8D%8A%E8%BA%AB%E5%83%8F_%E5%86%8A-089-%E5%85%AC%E7%BE%8A%E9%AB%98.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
